====================================================================================================
(c) DISCRIMINATION — DOES THE OBJECTION LAND WHERE THE DECISION WAS WRONG?
====================================================================================================
Overturn rate on objections to WRONG decisions minus the rate on objections to
RIGHT ones, on the same 896. An objection carrying no information should score
zero. jd4's reading was that a fabricated objection still discriminates because
its RECORD quotation is real, which is the half the judge checked.

arm                                         on WRONG (fixed|wrong)    on RIGHT (broken|right)     diff
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
M1 — real audit, OLD Step 1                          110/274 40.1%              128/622 20.6%    +19.6
jd5 B — real audit, NEW Step 1                       144/274 52.6%              167/622 26.8%    +25.7
jd4 — fabricated, OLD Step 1                          42/274 15.3%                49/622 7.9%     +7.5
jd5 A — fabricated, NEW Step 1                        29/274 10.6%                20/622 3.2%     +7.4
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Arm B's two rates BOTH rise. Arm A's both fall, and its difference barely moves —
the new Step 1 refuses fabricated objections roughly evenly on right and wrong
decisions, which is what a check on the objection rather than on the decision
should do.

====================================================================================================
(d) WHAT THE RULINGS SAY THEY DID  [KEYWORD INSTRUMENT — NOT AN INDEX COLUMN]
====================================================================================================
Regexes over the ruling's prose, printed in two readings because one regex is
one opinion: STRICT requires the absence to be asserted of a QUOTATION and in
the JUDGMENT, in one of four templates; BROAD accepts any absence cue in a
sentence that also names a quotation. The patterns are in
`RULING_LANGUAGE_PATTERNS` and `ruling_language()` and are re-derivable from a
run tree with `--scan-fabricated` / `--scan-real`.

THE CONTRAST IS WHAT THIS SUPPORTS, NOT THE ABSOLUTE RATE. A five-cell hand read
of arm B's strict hits found about half of them to be the phrase used about
something other than a `Judgment says:` quotation (CHECKLIST section 4).

arm                              names a MISSING quotation   (broad)    confirms PRESENT           essence
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
jd5 A — fabricated, NEW                      834/896 93.1%     95.6%       134/896 15.0%     194/896 21.7%
jd5 B — real audit, NEW                        27/896 3.0%      6.9%       412/896 46.0%     107/896 11.9%
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
An objection whose quotations are INVENTED gets an absence report; one whose
quotations are REAL does not. That is the check doing what it was written to do,
and it is the strongest single line of evidence that the paragraph was read.

THE RESIDUAL, AND IT IS THE SMOKE'S PARTIAL PASS SURVIVING AT SCALE. The new
paragraph forbids repairing the objection on its behalf. `essence` counts
rulings using 'essence', 'captures the' or 'paraphrase' anywhere:

                                 rulings           essence   overturns     essence among overturns
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
jd5 A — fabricated, NEW              896     194/896 21.7%          49                 11/49 22.4%
jd5 B — real audit, NEW              896     107/896 11.9%         311                 15/311 4.8%
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A fabricated objection that still moves a decision under the new prompt is, one
time in five, one the judge said it could not find and then ruled on anyway.

